"""Micras Bluetooth module: an HM-19 BLE module on a USB-C plug.

The module plugs into the main board's USB-C receptacle U2 (boards/main, PERIPHERALS.U2) and
uses it as a four-wire port:
- 3V3 from the main board (its 3V3 through a switch and the PTC fuse F1) on the plug's SBU1 and
  SBU2;
- GND on the plug's GND contacts and shell;
- UART on the USB data contacts: HM-19 UART_TX through R1 to D- (the main board's USBD_N, MCU
  PA11 = UART4_RX), HM-19 UART_RX through R2 to D+ (USBD_P, PA12 = UART4_TX). The plug joins
  both D+ and both D- contacts, and SBU1 with SBU2, so it mates the same way either side up.
VBUS of the plug is left open.

Signature (review M6): the plug's CC1 and CC2 contacts are on D+ (net UART_RX), copper only.
The main board's UART4 TX (PA12) idles high, so with the module plugged in it pulls the main
board's CC contacts, each held by its 5.1 kOhm Rd, up to the UART idle level, about 3.2 V. No
USB Type-C partner puts that level on a sink's CC: across the Rd a source makes at most 2.04 V
(Type-C R2.0 Table 4-36, vRd-Connect max with a +-10 % Rd; 2.18 V with +-20 %, Table 4-35), and
cables and accessories only add Ra or Rd to GND (Table 4-26, section 4.5.2.2). The main board
switches 3V3 onto SBU only while its CC contact holds a level above that, so a USB host, a cable
or an audio accessory never finds a supply on SBU (section 4.3, Table 4-28: SBU open). Both CC
contacts carry the signature, so it shows in either orientation; a TX byte pulls it low for at
most 9 bit times, which the main board's detector holds through. The load on PA12 is the two Rd
in parallel, 2.55 kOhm: 1.3 mA at 3.3 V.

Every part carries the designator of the MicrasBluetoothModule Altium design it replicates, and
boards/bluetooth/previous/board.json is that design's connectivity; `micras-pcb previous compare
bluetooth` checks this board against it and boards/bluetooth/changes.toml.

HM-19 application circuit (JNHuaMao HM Bluetooth module datasheet V107, section 6.5 schematic):
- R1, R2: 1 kOhm in series with UART_TX and UART_RX, as the vendor schematic. With the main
  board's 33 Ohm and 18 pF on each line the RC is about 24 ns, against 8.68 us per bit at the
  firmware's 115 200 baud.
- C1 10 uF and C2 100 nF on VCC (pins 9 and 10), as the vendor schematic.
- PIO1 (pin 15, "System LED", section 7) drives the blue LED D1 through R3 680 Ohm (the vendor
  uses 470 Ohm): about (3.3 V - 2.9 V) / 680 Ohm = 0.6 mA (SMLD12BN1W VF 2.9 V typ at 5 mA,
  ROHM SMLD12x Rev.005 p1; IF max 20 mA).
- PIO0 (pin 16) on the open pad OP1 to GND: the vendor schematic's KEY switch. Bridging it for
  over 1000 ms disconnects a connected module, and in standby restores the factory settings
  (section 7).
- R4 10 kOhm from RESETB (pin 11) to 3V3: the CC2640R2F's RESET_N has no internal pull-up
  (SWRS204C Table 7-1, "Reset, active-low. No internal pullup."), and the HM-19 datasheet (6.8)
  does not say whether the module adds one. RESETB is active low ("Reset if low >1000ms", 6.8);
  R4 holds it at 3V3, above VIH = 0.8 VDDS (SWRS204C 8.25).
- RTS, CTS, PIO2, PIO3 and KEY (pin 8) are open, as in the vendor schematic.

Mechanical source of truth: spec/bluetooth.toml (outline extents, plug position, thickness,
mating with the main board's U2).
"""

load("@stdlib/interfaces.zen", "DiffPair", "Uart", "Usb2")
load(
    "@stdlib/board_config.zen",
    "BoardConfig",
    "Constraints",
    "Copper",
    "CopperLayer",
    "DesignRules",
    "DielectricLayer",
    "Holes",
    "Material",
    "NetClass",
    "PredefinedSizes",
    "Silkscreen",
    "SolderMask",
    "Stackup",
    "ViaDimension",
)

Resistor = Module("@stdlib/generics/Resistor.zen")
Capacitor = Module("@stdlib/generics/Capacitor.zen")
Led = Module("@stdlib/generics/Led.zen")
HM19 = Module("github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/jnhuamao/HM-19/HM-19.zen")
PLUG = Module("github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/jing-extension/918-118A2021Y40006/918-118A2021Y40006.zen")
OpenPad = Module("github.com/Team-Micras/MicrasPCB/components/micras/OpenPad/OpenPad.zen")

# The main board's 3V3 through its SBU switch and F1, on the plug's SBU contacts.
V3V3 = Power("3V3", voltage="3.3V")
GND = Ground("GND")

# Plug side of the UART: D- carries the module's TX, D+ its RX (names as on the Altium design).
# UART_RX, the main board's TX, also carries the CC1/CC2 signature.
UART_TX = Net("UART_TX")
UART_RX = Net("UART_RX")
RESETB = Net("RESETB")
# Module side of R1 and R2.
MODULE_UART = Uart(TX=Net("HM19_TX"), RX=Net("HM19_RX"))
LED_PIO = Net("LED_PIO")
LED_A = Net("LED_A")
KEY = Net("KEY")

# 1 % 0603 thick film, 100 mW 75 V (UNI-ROYAL 0603WAF series, JLCPCB basic parts).
R_1K = {"value": "1kOhm 1%", "package": "0603", "power": "100mW", "mpn": "0603WAF1001T5E", "manufacturer": "UNI-ROYAL"}
R_10K = {
    "value": "10kOhm 1%",
    "package": "0603",
    "power": "100mW",
    "mpn": "0603WAF1002T5E",
    "manufacturer": "UNI-ROYAL",
}
R_680 = {
    "value": "680Ohm 1%",
    "package": "0603",
    "power": "100mW",
    "mpn": "0603WAF6800T5E",
    "manufacturer": "UNI-ROYAL",
}

HM19(
    name="U1",
    VCC=V3V3,
    GND=GND,
    UART=MODULE_UART,
    RTS=NotConnected(),
    CTS=NotConnected(),
    KEY=NotConnected(),
    RESETB=RESETB,
    PIO0=KEY,
    PIO1=LED_PIO,
    PIO2=NotConnected(),
    PIO3=NotConnected(),
)

PLUG(
    name="USB1",
    VBUS=NotConnected(),
    GND=GND,
    SHELL=GND,
    CC1=UART_RX,
    CC2=UART_RX,
    SBU1=V3V3,
    SBU2=V3V3,
    USB=Usb2(D=DiffPair(P=UART_RX, N=UART_TX)),
)

Resistor(name="R1", P1=UART_TX, P2=MODULE_UART.TX, **R_1K)
Resistor(name="R2", P1=UART_RX, P2=MODULE_UART.RX, **R_1K)
Resistor(name="R4", P1=RESETB, P2=V3V3, **R_10K)

Capacitor(
    name="C1",
    P1=GND,
    P2=V3V3,
    value="10uF 10%",
    package="0603",
    voltage="35V",
    dielectric="X5R",
    mpn="HGC0603R5106K350NTHJ",
    manufacturer="Chinocera",
)
Capacitor(
    name="C2",
    P1=GND,
    P2=V3V3,
    value="100nF 10%",
    package="0603",
    voltage="50V",
    dielectric="X7R",
    mpn="CC0603KRX7R9BB104",
    manufacturer="YAGEO",
)

Resistor(name="R3", P1=LED_PIO, P2=LED_A, **R_680)
Led(
    name="D1",
    A=LED_A,
    K=GND,
    package="0603",
    color="blue",
    forward_voltage="2.9V",
    mpn="SMLD12BN1WT86",
    manufacturer="ROHM",
)

OpenPad(name="OP1", P1=KEY, P2=GND)

# Stackup and rules from the Altium MicrasBluetoothModule.PcbDoc (layer stack and design rules):
# 2 layers, 35 um copper on a 0.7 mm FR-4 core (Dk 4.5). Zener writes a 10 um solder mask a side
# (not configurable): 0.79 mm; Altium's 0.5 mil (12.7 um) resist makes it 0.7954 mm. Rules (the
# Altium JLCPCB rule set): 0.1 mm clearance, 10 mil (0.254 mm) track, 0.4 mm / 0.3 mm minimum via,
# 0.3 mm minimum hole, 0.254 mm hole to hole, 0.2 mm copper to board edge, 0.02 mm solder mask
# expansion (rule SolderMaskExpansion, 0.7874 mil). The routing uses 0.254 mm tracks and
# 0.6 / 0.3 mm and 0.5 / 0.3 mm vias. Silkscreen text: 0.5 mm minimum height, the size of the
# reference designators (0.5 mm, 0.15 mm stroke), which the team reads on the previous boards;
# JLCPCB recommends 1.0 mm, so text this small may print less sharply.
Board(
    name="bluetooth",
    path="layout",
    layers=2,
    schematic=True,
    config=BoardConfig(
        design_rules=DesignRules(
            constraints=Constraints(
                copper=Copper(
                    minimum_clearance=0.1,
                    minimum_track_width=0.1,
                    minimum_via_diameter=0.4,
                    copper_to_edge_clearance=0.2,
                ),
                holes=Holes(minimum_through_hole=0.3, hole_to_hole_clearance=0.254),
                silkscreen=Silkscreen(minimum_text_height=0.5),
                solder_mask=SolderMask(clearance=0.02),
            ),
            predefined_sizes=PredefinedSizes(
                track_widths=[0.254],
                via_dimensions=[
                    ViaDimension(diameter=0.6, drill=0.3),
                    ViaDimension(diameter=0.5, drill=0.3),
                ],
            ),
            netclasses=[
                NetClass(
                    name="Default",
                    clearance=0.1,
                    track_width=0.254,
                    via_diameter=0.6,
                    via_drill=0.3,
                ),
            ],
        ),
        stackup=Stackup(
            materials=[Material(name="FR-4", relative_permittivity=4.5)],
            thickness=0.79,
            layers=[
                CopperLayer(thickness=0.035, role="mixed"),
                DielectricLayer(thickness=0.7, material="FR-4", form="core"),
                CopperLayer(thickness=0.035, role="mixed"),
            ],
        ),
    ),
)
